.TH "UBERTOOTH\-BTLE" "1" "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "User Commands"
.SH "NAME"
\fBubertooth\-btle\fR \- Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) sniffing and more
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.nf
ubertooth\-btle \-f [\-A 37|38|39] [\-r output\.pcapng]
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
\fBubertooth\-btle\fR is a tool for doing Fun Stuff(TM) with BLE\. It can do the following things:
.IP "\(bu" 4
Sniff connections
.IP "\(bu" 4
Interfere with connections
.IP "\(bu" 4
Send advertising packets (experimental)
.IP "" 0
.P
Sniffing connections is the most robust feature supported by \fBubertooth\-btle\fR\. It has two primary modes of operation: follow mode and promiscuous mode\.
.P
Follow mode is the preferred mode for general use\. In this mode, Ubertooth will listen on one of three advertising channels waiting for a BLE connection to be established\. When a connection is established, Ubertooth will hop along the data channels, passively capturing the data sent between the central and peripheral\. After the connection terminates, Ubertooth will return to the advertising channel and wait for another connection\.
.P
No\-follow mode is similar to follow mode, but it only logs advertising packets and will not follow connections as they are established\.
.P
Promiscuous mode is an experimental mode for sniffing connections after they have already been established\. This mode can be used to sniff long\-lived connections\.
.P
When sniffing, Ubertooth can only operate in either follow mode or promiscuous mode, but not both at the same time\. If you are unsure which mode to use, use follow mode\.
.P
While promiscuous mode hunts for an access address it is gated by the board's squelch; follow and no\-follow mode are not\. \fBubertooth\-btle\fR does not set the squelch, so it is the default \-120 dBm unless \fBubertooth\-util \-z\fR set another value while the board was idle, and the firmware resets it on every mode change, so it never carries over from an earlier capture\. See ubertooth\-util(1), SQUELCH\.
.P
By default, Ubertooth will follow any connection it observes\. You can limit this to following a specific Bluetooth Address (BD ADDR) using the \fB\-t\fR command line flag\. For example, the following command will only sniff connections where the central or peripheral's BD ADDR is \fB22:44:66:88:AA:CC\fR:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-btle \-f \-t 22:44:66:88:AA:CC
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
\fB\-t\fR can also take a mask length in CIDR\-like notation\. Masks can be between 1 and 48 bits long, with a 48 bit mask matching the entire address\. Using a /24 mask will filter on just the OUI\. For example, to limit sniffing to just TI devices with the OUI 00:1A:7D, use the following:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-btle \-t 00:1A:7D:00:00:00/24
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Filters persist until they are explicitly cleared or the system restarts (either via \fBubertooth\-util \-r\fR or unplug/replug)\. To clear a filter, use the special filter \fBnone\fR\. Example:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-btle \-t none
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
When filtering, previous versions of the firmware would still log all advertising packets but only follow connections based on the filter parameters\. As of 2018\-06\-R1, advertising packets that do not match the filter are dropped\.
.P
In all sniffing modes, Ubertooth can log data to PCAP or PcapNG with a variety of pseudoheaders\. The recommended logging format is PcapNG (\fB\-r\fR) or PCAP with LE Pseudoheader (\fB\-q\fR)\. For compatibility with crackle (see \fIUSING WITH CRACKLE\fR), use PCAP with PPI (\fB\-c\fR)\.
.P
In every sniffing mode, a packet carrying a Bluetooth 4\.2 Data Length Extension payload, up to its 251\-byte maximum, is captured whole: on the screen, in PCAP and in PCAPNG\. The board sends such a packet in several fixed\-size records, which are put back together before anything is decoded or written\. Older firmware cut every packet to 50 bytes\.
.P
Interfering with connections is a feature for causing intentional interference with newly established or long\-lived connections\. When this attack succeeds, the BLE connection between the central and peripheral will be terminated\. Pair the \fB\-i\fR or \fB\-I\fR flag with \fB\-f\fR to interfere with new connections or \fB\-p\fR to interfere with long\-lived connections\. \fB\-i\fR or \fB\-I\fR (or their older spellings \fB\-j\fR and \fB\-J\fR) with neither \fB\-f\fR nor \fB\-p\fR is refused before any device is opened: there is no connection to interfere with\. Note that causing intentional interference may be illegal in your jurisdiction\. Check your local laws before using this feature\.
.P
Finally, \fBubertooth\-btle\fR supports transmitting advertising packets with a specified BD ADDR\. This feature, referred to as faux slave mode, is experimental and may not function as intended\. Use at your own risk\.
.SH "EXAMPLES"
Sniff all connections on advertising channel 38, logging all data to PcapNG:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-btle \-f \-A 38 \-r log\.pcapng
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Log advertising packets without following connections:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-btle \-n
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Interfere with connections recovered with promiscuous mode:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-btle \-p \-I
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Send advertising packets using BD ADDR \fB22:44:66:88:AA:CC\fR:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-btle \-s 22:44:66:88:AA:CC
.fi
.IP "" 0
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-h\fR
Displays help message
.P
Major modes:
.TP
\fB\-f\fR
Follow mode: sniff connections as they are established
.TP
\fB\-n\fR
No\-follow mode: log advertising packets but don't follow connections
.TP
\fB\-p\fR
Promiscuous mode: sniff already\-established connections\. As it works a connection out it reports each parameter it has recovered: the access address, the CRC init value, the hop interval and the hop increment\. Those lines go to standard output, or to standard error once \fB\-q \-\fR or \fB\-c \-\fR has taken standard output, so a capture written to a file still says which connection it is of\.
.TP
\fB\-s <BD ADDR>\fR
Inject advertising packets using specified BD ADDR\. Like \fB\-a\fR, it does not go with \fB\-f\fR, \fB\-n\fR or \fB\-p\fR\. It takes the same spellings \fB\-t\fR does, and advertises from the address it was given: the \fB/<bits>\fR mask and \fBnone\fR mean nothing to a faux slave, so \fB\-s none\fR advertises from \fB00:00:00:00:00:00\fR\.
.P
Interference (only with \fB\-f\fR or \fB\-p\fR):
.TP
\fB\-i\fR
Interfere with one connection and return to idle
.TP
\fB\-I\fR
Interfere continuously with many connections
.TP
\fB\-j\fR
Original "jam" spelling of \fB\-i\fR; does exactly what \fB\-i\fR does
.TP
\fB\-J\fR
Original "jam" spelling of \fB\-I\fR; does exactly what \fB\-I\fR does
.P
Filtering:
.TP
\fB\-t <BD ADDR>\fR
Limit connection following and interference in follow mode to the specified BD ADDR\. \fB\-s\fR and \fB\-t\fR are two different addresses and a command can give both: \fB\-s A \-t B\fR advertises from A and follows B, and \fB\-t none\fR clears the target without touching the address \fB\-s\fR was given\. They shared one address, so whichever of the two was written last used to set both, and \fB\-s <BD ADDR> \-t none\fR advertised from \fB00:00:00:00:00:00\fR without a word\.
.P
Logging:
.TP
\fB\-r <output\.pcapng>\fR
Log to PcapNG (preferred)\. PcapNG output cannot be streamed to stdout because the writer must seek while finalizing the file; \fB\-r \-\fR is rejected\.
.TP
\fB\-q <output\.pcap|\->\fR
Log to PCAP with \fBDLT_BLUETOOTH_LE_LL_WITH_PHDR\fR\. Use \fB\-\fR to stream binary PCAP to stdout\.
.TP
\fB\-c <output\.pcap|\->\fR
Log to PCAP with PPI (for compatibility with crackle(1))\. Use \fB\-\fR to stream binary PCAP to stdout, for example:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-btle \-f \-c \- | wireshark \-k \-i \-
.fi
.IP "" 0
.IP
When stdout capture is selected, regular packet/status text is suppressed so it cannot corrupt the binary stream; errors remain on stderr\. Such a run does not produce the per\-packet dump at all, so the decoder's complaints about an advertising field it could not read in one of those packets do not reach stderr either\. The lines that answer an option in the same command go to stderr rather than being suppressed with it: the \fBAccess address:\fR line of \fB\-a\fR, the \fBCRC:\fR line of \fB\-v\fR, the target lines of \fB\-t\fR, and the connection parameters \fB\-p\fR recovers\. The tool refuses to write binary capture data directly to an interactive terminal: \fBerror: stdout is a tty, refusing to write binary capture data to it\fR, with exit status 1 and before any device is opened\.
.P
The times in a logged capture follow the board's capture clock, which starts over every 429\.50 s in the \fB\-f\fR and \fB\-n\fR modes and every 327\.68 s in promiscuous mode\. A promiscuous packet carries a count of how often it has, which covers a quiet gap; the packets of the other two modes carry none, so a gap longer than one period leaves every later time short by a period\. That cannot be recovered, but it is reported, on standard error: \fBwarning: packet times are behind the host clock by more than half the capture clock's 429\.50 s period: a quiet gap lost a clock wrap, or the host clock was stepped forwards\fR\. Both causes look the same from here, which is why the message names each\.
.P
Miscellaneous:
.TP
\fB\-A <37|38|39>\fR
Which advertising channel to use in follow mode (default: 37)
.TP
\fB\-a [address]\fR
Get (\fB\-a\fR) or set (\fB\-a <address>\fR) the access address used in promiscuous mode\. The address is 1 to 8 hex digits (\fB\-a 8e89bed6\fR); fewer than 8 means leading zeros (\fB\-a 12345\fR sets \fB00012345\fR)\. Anything else is refused before the device is opened\. Getting and setting in one command is refused; other options such as \fB\-v\fR still run after a get\. \fB\-a\fR does not go with a sniffing mode (\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-n\fR, \fB\-p\fR), which runs until it is interrupted; the combination is refused before any device is opened\.
.TP
\fB\-v [01]\fR
Read (\fB\-v\fR) or set (\fB\-v 0\fR or \fB\-v 1\fR) CRC verification (default: 0)\. Any other value is refused\. A set and a read in one command both happen, the set first, so the read reports the new value, in whichever order they are written; two sets are refused, as two values for one setting are everywhere (ubertooth(7), OPTION VALUES)\. In a sniffing mode both run once the mode has started, because entering a mode clears the setting, and the firmware then turns verification on and off itself as it recovers a connection\.
.TP
\fB\-x <0\-32>\fR
Allow n access address violations (default: 32)\. Filtering occurs on host\.
.P
Data source:
.TP
\fB\-U <index|serial>\fR
select an Ubertooth by enumeration index or serial number; see ubertooth(7), SELECTING A DEVICE
.SH "USING WITH CRACKLE"
\fBcrackle\fR is a tool for cracking the BLE key exchange and decrypting encrypted data\. To capture data for use with \fBcrackle\fR, sniff connections in follow mode using \fB\-f\fR and log data to PCAP/PPI using \fB\-c\fR\. Example:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-btle \-f \-c crack\.pcap
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Refer to \fBcrackle\fR documentation for further details\.
.SH "TRANSMIT POWER"
Injecting advertising packets (\fB\-s\fR) and interference (\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-I\fR, \fB\-j\fR, \fB\-J\fR) transmit at the CC2400 power level set with \fBubertooth\-util \-a\fR and leave it unchanged\. A board that has not been given a level transmits at 7 (0 dBm), the CC2400's reset value\. Sniffing does not transmit and does not change the level\. These levels are at the CC2400 radio; the Ubertooth One's RF amplifier changes the actual output after that\.
.SH "ARGUMENTS"
\fBubertooth\-btle\fR takes options only\. A word that is not an option, including a bare \fB\-\fR, is refused before any device is opened and named: \fBerror: unexpected argument '<word>'\fR, with exit status 1\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR ends the command only once the whole command line has parsed, so a stray word or a bad option value is reported even alongside it\.
.P
An option value must be a whole value in its range, or it is refused the same way instead of being read as 0, for example \fBerror: advertising channel index must be 37\-39 (got 40)\fR\.
.P
A bare \fB\-\fR is still the stdin/stdout value of \fB\-q\fR, \fB\-c\fR and \fB\-r\fR\. See ubertooth(7), OPTION VALUES, for the forms an option's value can take, including the optional values of \fB\-a\fR and \fB\-v\fR\.
.P
An option combination the tool cannot carry out is refused there too, before any device is opened, so the message never depends on which boards are attached: more than one of \fB\-f\fR, \fB\-n\fR and \fB\-p\fR gives \fBerror: choose one of \-f, \-n or \-p\fR, and a read and a set of the access address in one command gives \fBerror: \-a cannot both read and set the access address\fR\.
.P
\fB\-a\fR or \fB\-s\fR alongside a sniffing mode gives \fBerror: \-a and \-s do not go with \-f, \-n or \-p: a sniffing mode runs until it is interrupted\fR, so there is nothing for an access address or faux slave mode request to run after\. \fB\-t\fR and \fB\-v\fR are not in that list: \fB\-t\fR is sent before the mode starts, which is what it is for, and the \fB\-v\fR options run once it has started\.
.P
\fB\-i\fR or \fB\-I\fR without \fB\-f\fR or \fB\-p\fR gives \fBerror: \-i needs \-f or \-p: there is no connection to interfere with otherwise\fR, naming the spelling that was used (\fB\-j\fR and \fB\-J\fR included)\.
.P
That includes \fB\-i\fR on its own: it says more than listing every major mode would, because only two of them can carry interference\.
.P
A command line with no work in it is refused the same way\. \fB\-U\fR, \fB\-A\fR, \fB\-x\fR and the capture options only shape work another option asks for, so a command made up of those alone, or a bare \fBubertooth\-btle\fR, gives \fBerror: nothing to do: choose a major mode (\-f, \-n or \-p), or \-a, \-v, \-s or \-t\fR, on standard error with nothing on standard output\. A bare \fBubertooth\-btle\fR is the one refusal the usage text follows, also on standard error; every other refused command line is the \fBerror:\fR line alone, and \fB\-h\fR prints the usage text on standard output\.
.P
A refused command line creates and truncates nothing: \fB\-r\fR, \fB\-q\fR and \fB\-c\fR name their file while the command line is read, and it is opened only once every check has passed\.
.SH "DIAGNOSTICS"
Standard output carries only what was asked for: either the decoded packets or the capture data of \fB\-q \-\fR or \fB\-c \-\fR, plus the lines that answer \fB\-a\fR, \fB\-v\fR and \fB\-t\fR and the parameters \fB\-p\fR recovers\. With the capture on standard output, standard output belongs to the capture for the whole run: those lines move to standard error, and the decoded packets are not printed at all\.
.P
Errors, warnings and progress go to standard error, each error as one line starting \fBerror:\fR, for example \fBerror: USB poll failed\fR when the board stops answering during a capture, \fBerror: jamming not supported by this firmware\fR, \fBerror: target set failed\fR, or \fBerror: target address must be xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx (got 11:22:33:44:55)\fR\. A value that is not an address reads the same way whatever is wrong with it, and names the address the option sets, so \fB\-s\fR gives \fBerror: faux slave address must be xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx (got <word>)\fR and a bad mask gives \fBerror: target mask must be 0\-48 (got 49)\fR\. A capture file that cannot be created is reported as \fBerror: <file>: <reason>\fR\.
.P
A capture that is losing packets says so\. This tool reads its packets one at a time, and a reply that held part of a record cannot be read as one, so it is dropped and reported once per run: \fBwarning: a poll reply arrived with 32 of a packet's 64 bytes and was dropped; the capture is losing packets\fR\. The capture runs on and ends as it otherwise would, so the warning is the only sign\.
.P
Each request that sets a mode up is named on its own, so the line says which one the board refused: \fBerror: could not set the advertising channel\fR, \fBerror: could not set the advertising data\fR, \fBerror: could not start advertising\fR for \fB\-s\fR, and \fBerror: could not start follow mode\fR (or \fBno\-follow\fR, or \fBpromiscuous\fR) for the sniffing modes\. No capture starts after one of these\.
.P
\fB\-q\fR and \fB\-c\fR share one capture handle, so a second file between them is ignored with \fBwarning: ignoring extra capture file: <name>\fR and the first one given is used; \fB\-r\fR has its own handle, so \fB\-q a\.pcap \-r b\.pcapng\fR writes both\.
.P
A command line the tool will not take is reported as that one \fBerror:\fR line on standard error, with no usage text after it: the line describes the problem, and \fB\-h\fR prints the usage text for a reader who wants it\. An option the tool does not know reads \fBerror: unrecognized option '\-Z'\fR; one whose value is missing reads \fBerror: option '\-A' requires a value\fR\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR is what the user asked for, so the usage text goes to standard output\. Nothing else goes to standard error either, unless the rest of the command line produced a warning before \fB\-h\fR ended the command\. A bare \fBubertooth\-btle\fR, with no arguments at all, is the one refusal the usage text does follow, on standard error with the \fBerror:\fR line\.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 when the command did what was asked, including \fB\-h\fR, and after a capture is interrupted (Ctrl\-C) having polled cleanly to that point\.
.P
1 for a command line the tool will not take or with nothing to do, for a capture file that cannot be created, when the selected Ubertooth is not found or cannot be opened, when the board refuses the jam mode, a \fB\-t\fR target, a sniffing or faux slave mode, or an access address or CRC verify request, and when a capture stops short because a poll failed\. No device is opened for a refused command line\.
